## 1. Behavioral-delta enumeration (old lowercase-trimmed-Description exact
key vs new normalize+subset-merge, re-keyed by groupDisplayLabel)

Verified with a throwaway `go run` harness calling merchants.Normalize +
GroupKeys directly (not part of the diff, discarded after use):

- "PAYPAL INST XFER 0612" vs "PAYPAL INST XFER 0712" -> both normalize to
  "PAYPAL INST XFER" (digit token dropped) -> MERGE under new, stayed split
  under old (exact full-string match). This is a genuine delta and an
  intended improvement: exactly the class of case Normalize's digit-drop
  rule exists for (ANALYTICS_PORT_SPEC.md §2). Not a regression.
- "AMZN MKTP US*1A2B3C" vs "AMZN MKTP US*4D5E6F" -> order ID is fused into
  one alnum token (not all-digit), survives Normalize -> stays SPLIT under
  new, exactly as it would have under old (old also matched full description
  strings exactly, so these were never merged before either). No delta —
  confirmed empirically, not just reasoned.
- handlers.go:326 (`merchants.GroupTransactions(outflows.Transactions)`) now
  keys off DisplayName-first-then-Description, whereas the old code
  (`strings.ToLower(strings.TrimSpace(t.Description))`, removed in this
  diff) used Description only. This is the single largest delta in the
  change and is spec-mandated (ANALYTICS_PORT_SPEC.md §3: "key merchants off
  DisplayName falling back to Description"), not an accidental side effect —
  flagging so the lead is aware it's real and load-bearing, not incidental.
- Multi-token near-duplicates (store-number/city suffixes, "ACME COFFEE" /
  "ACME COFFEE STORE #12") now merge via the ruled subset rule where old
  relied only on exact-match + mergeSimilarGroups' narrow domain-suffix
  heuristic. Intended improvement per the ruled P1 standard, pinned by
  merchants_test.go's diamond test (TestGroupKeys_DiamondSubsetMergesIntoOneGroup,
  merchants_test.go:318).
- Degenerate single-token descriptions (bare "SQ", any bare one-word
  merchant) still require exact string equality to merge — unchanged,
  conservative, no regression.

Net: every delta found either (a) reproduces old behavior exactly (AMZN
case) or (b) is a documented, spec-ruled improvement (digit-drop,
subset-merge, DisplayName-first). No accidental split-what-old-merged or
merge-what-old-split case found.

## 2. mergeSimilarGroups re-keying / display-label collision

Proved (not just spot-checked) that two DIFFERENT canonical groups cannot
produce an identical groupDisplayLabel: groupDisplayLabel(raw) =
ToLower(TrimSpace(raw)); two raw strings equal after that transform differ
only in case and/or leading/trailing whitespace. merchants.Normalize also
uppercases and trims (Fields/Join), so any two raw strings that are
lower-trim-equal are *also* Normalize-equal, meaning they'd already collapse
to the same normalized key inside GroupTransactions (rawKeys[i] =
Normalize(raw), unioned via the literal-equality branch of shouldMerge
before any subset logic even runs) — i.e. they were never two different
canonical groups to begin with. Label collision across genuinely distinct
canonical clusters is structurally impossible here (mod exotic Unicode
case-folding asymmetries, not realistic for bank-export ASCII descriptions).

Real (but pre-existing, not introduced by this diff) permissiveness:
mergeSimilarGroups' HasPrefix-after-domain-strip heuristic
(handlers.go:243-266, unchanged) can still merge a canonical cluster whose
label is e.g. "starbucks store 12345" with a *separate*, degenerate-guard-
protected cluster labeled "starbucks" (HasPrefix("starbucks store
12345","starbucks") == true). This looks alarming in isolation but is NOT
new: under the old per-description-exact-key scheme the same two strings
would have been separate buckets subjected to the exact same prefix test
with the exact same outcome. Confirmed this is inherited, not introduced,
by inspection of the pre-diff grouping (one bucket per distinct
lower-trimmed Description, same mergeSimilarGroups call). This is exactly
what the 2026-08-12 ruling calls "orthogonal, preserves existing behavior."

One second-order case the ruling's language doesn't fully cover: because
canonical clustering now runs *before* mergeSimilarGroups and represents an
entire cluster by only its majority-raw label, a minority raw-description
variant inside a multi-token cluster (one absorbed into the cluster via the
subset rule, e.g. "RIDE CO EXPRESS" folded into a "RIDE CO"-labeled cluster)
loses its own identity as a standalone key for mergeSimilarGroups' pairwise
prefix test. In a contrived case where that specific minority variant (not
the majority label) is the string that would prefix-match some other
cluster's label, old would merge and new would not — a narrow, real
possibility, no regression test covers it, and it can only ever cause a
*missed* merge (fail toward separation), which is the safer failure
direction for recurring-detection quality. Flagging as a finding, not a
defect: no evidence it manifests in the existing 99-test fixture set or the
Lucid regression test, and erring toward under-merging is consistent with
the guard's own stated purpose (avoid bridging unrelated merchants).

## 3. pricecreep correctness

- pricecreep.go:99-100: firstMedian/lastMedian computed via sort.Slice by
  Date.Before, then hard slices [:3] and [len-3:]. Median-of-exactly-3
  resolves to the middle element (pricecreep.go:161-163), correct.
- PctChange formula (pricecreep.go:108): `(lastMedian-firstMedian)/
  firstMedian*100` — denominator is the *first*-window median (the
  historical baseline), consistent with "price crept up from X" framing.
  Correct and matches the doc comment.
- Threshold (pricecreep.go:109): `if pct <= creepThresholdPct { continue }`
  — strictly greater than 5% required to report, matching spec's "increases
  > 5%" exactly (not >=). Verified by TestDetect_GenuineStepAtMinimumOccurrences
  and there's no boundary-exactly-5% test, but the strict-`<=` skip is
  unambiguous from the source.
- Zero-baseline guard (pricecreep.go:102-106): skips rather than divides by
  zero — correct, no test exercises firstMedian==0 but the code path is
  simple and obviously correct by inspection.
- Same-day tie-break (pricecreep.go:95-97): `sort.Slice` (NOT
  `sort.SliceStable`), no secondary sort key. For a group with same-day
  duplicate transactions (e.g. split charges), which rows land in "first 3"
  vs the middle vs "last 3" is not guaranteed by Go's sort.Slice contract —
  it happens to be deterministic for a fixed Go toolchain and fixed input
  order (GroupTransactions preserves original transaction-set order, itself
  presumably file/load order), but this is unspecified behavior relied on
  implicitly rather than an explicit, documented tie-break like the outer
  creeps-level sort has (pricecreep.go:126-134, which explicitly documents
  its tie-break chain). TestDetect_Deterministic (pricecreep_test.go:210)
  only proves repeat-call determinism on one fixed input, not tie-break
  correctness under same-day duplicates — no test constructs that case.
  Worth a follow-up (sort.SliceStable, or an explicit secondary key such as
  transaction ID) but not a material defect: it doesn't misreport creep
  direction or magnitude, only which of several same-day rows anchors a
  window boundary in a rare input shape.

## 4. Cross-package filter consistency (P2 vs P3)

anomalies.go:161 filters `t.TransactionType == models.Outflow && t.Amount <
0`; pricecreep.go:77 filters `t.Amount < 0` only (no TransactionType check),
confirmed by TestDetect_ExpensesOnly (pricecreep_test.go:242) which only
proves positive-Amount Income rows are excluded, not that a negative-Amount
non-Outflow row would be. This is the exact inconsistency the 2026-08-12
ruling names explicitly ("(3) expense filter ruled... applies to anomalies
(P2) and pricecreep (P5 fix)") and defers to P5. Per the task instructions,
ruled/deferred items are findings, not blockers — logged, not re-litigated.
User-visible impact in the interim: a hypothetical negative-Amount
transaction tagged something other than Outflow (e.g. a mis-typed refund)
could be picked up by pricecreep's creep math but excluded from anomalies'
peer baseline — low practical likelihood given app-native data conventions,
worth confirming at P5 time.

